<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Geoffrey Chaucer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1343–<a href="/years/1400">1400</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Geoffrey Chaucer was an English poet and royal official, often called the father of English literature. Working as a courtier, diplomat and customs controller in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, he wrote Troilus and Criseyde and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-canterbury-tales">The Canterbury Tales</a> in the London dialect of Middle English, helping to make English a literary language alongside French and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>. He was buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/geoffrey-chaucer.jpg" alt="Geoffrey Chaucer" width="320" height="437" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chaucer_manuscrit_portrait_(d%C3%A9tail).jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Geoffrey Chaucer was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, most likely in the early 1340s; his monument gives 1343, but the precise date and place are unknown. His father, John Chaucer, rose to be an important wine merchant with a royal appointment, and earlier generations of the family had been vintners and merchants in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ipswich">Ipswich</a>. The surname derives from the French chaucier, a maker of shoes, boots or leggings. In 1357 the first of the Chaucer Life Records shows him as a page in the household of Elizabeth de Burgh, Countess of Ulster, whose husband Lionel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> was a son of Edward III; the post brought him into the court circle in which he stayed for the rest of his life.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Chaucer was a public servant whose official life is very well documented, with nearly 500 written items. He worked as a courtier, a diplomat and a civil servant, sat in the Parliament of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> as shire knight for Kent, and was Clerk of the King's Works from 1389 to 1391. He was a close friend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-gaunt">John of Gaunt, Duke of Lancaster</a>, and served under his patronage; in 1366 he married Philippa de Roet, and when Lancaster married her sister Katherine Swynford in 1396 the two men became brothers-in-law.</p><p>His first major work was The Book of the Duchess, an elegy for Blanche of Lancaster, who died in 1368. He wrote many of his major works while he was customs comptroller for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, from 1374 to 1386, among them the Parlement of Foules, The Legend of Good Women and Troilus and Criseyde, and he is believed to have begun <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-canterbury-tales">The Canterbury Tales</a> in the 1380s. He also translated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boethius">Boethius</a>'s Consolation of Philosophy and wrote A Treatise on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astrolabe">Astrolabe</a> for his ten-year-old son Lewis.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Chaucer died on 25 October 1400 and was the first writer buried in what has since become Poets' Corner in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a>. He invented the rhyme royal and was one of the first English poets to use the five-stress line. His contemporary Thomas Hoccleve called him the first finder of our fair language, and almost two thousand English words are first attested in his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>. Eighty-three manuscripts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-canterbury-tales">The Canterbury Tales</a> survive, in whole or part, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-caxton">William Caxton</a>, the first English printer, published two folio editions of it, in 1478 and 1483.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Geoffrey Chaucer” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The English language, transformed after 1066 by French vocabulary, became a major literary language with <b>Geoffrey Chaucer</b>’s Canterbury Tales in the late fourteenth century, and William Caxton printed the first books in England in the 1470s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Geoffrey Chaucer</b> died, according to the date on his tomb in Westminster Abbey.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-25">25 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1380" aria-label="London on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4547 381 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Geoffrey Chaucer"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4997 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4997 651h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1380">What was here in 1380? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Whan that Aprille with his shoures sote The droghte of Marche hath perced to the rote, And bathed every veyne in swich licour, Of which vertu engendred is the flour…&#8221;</p><footer>The Canterbury Tales, General Prologue (the opening) &#183; original &#183; Middle English, W. W. Skeat's edition (1894) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/22120/pg22120-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;He never yet no vileinye ne sayde In al his lyf, un-to no maner wight. He was a verray parfit gentil knight.&#8221;</p><footer>The Canterbury Tales, General Prologue (the Knight) &#183; original &#183; Middle English, W. W. Skeat's edition (1894) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/22120/pg22120-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;And gladly wolde he lerne, and gladly teche.&#8221;</p><footer>The Canterbury Tales, General Prologue (the Clerk) &#183; original &#183; Middle English, W. W. Skeat's edition (1894) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/22120/pg22120-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Mordre wol out, certein, it wol nat faille, And namely ther thonour of god shal sprede, The blood out cryeth on your cursed dede.&#8221;</p><footer>The Canterbury Tales, the Prioress's Tale &#183; original &#183; spoken by the Prioress in her tale &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/22120/pg22120-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;For o thing, sires, saufly dar I seye, That frendes everich other moot obeye, If they wol longe holden companye. Love wol nat ben constreyned by maistrye…&#8221;</p><footer>The Canterbury Tales, the Franklin's Tale &#183; original &#183; spoken by the Franklin &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/22120/pg22120-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Whan maistrie comth, the god of love anon Beteth hise winges, and farewel! he is gon! Love is a thing as any spirit free…&#8221;</p><footer>The Canterbury Tales, the Franklin's Tale &#183; original &#183; spoken by the Franklin &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/22120/pg22120-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
